result_text:
Eight findings from the `cc0e53e...a6a4623` diff. The top two break things: randomized test failures and mixed-version cluster failures. Nothing was verified, per the review contract. None of the eight is a CLAUDE.md violation: the user-level file only covers tooling workflow, and this repo has no CLAUDE.md of its own.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "server/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/search/fetch/subphase/highlight/HighlightBuilderTests.java",
    "line": 580,
    "summary": "testInvalidMaxAnalyzedOffset still picks from randomIntBetween(-100, 0), but -1 and 0 are now valid values, so the expected parse exception is sometimes not thrown.",
    "failure_scenario": "When the random value is -1 or 0 (about 2 in 101 runs), the builder accepts it, expectParseThrows fails, and the test becomes flaky in CI. The range should be randomIntBetween(-100, -2) or similar."
  },
  {
    "file": "server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/search/fetch/subphase/highlight/AbstractHighlighterBuilder.java",
    "line": 168,
    "summary": "The new values -1 and 0 are sent over the wire with no TransportVersion check, and older nodes run the old validation (<= 0 throws) inside readFrom when they deserialize.",
    "failure_scenario": "In a mixed-version cluster (for example during a rolling upgrade), an upgraded coordinating node sends a shard search request with max_analyzed_offset=-1 to an old data node. The old node's StreamInput constructor calls maxAnalyzedOffset(-1), throws IllegalArgumentException('must be a positive integer'), and the shard requests fail instead of falling back to the index setting."
  },
  {
    "file": "rest-api-spec/src/yamlRestTest/resources/rest-api-spec/test/search.highlight/30_max_analyzed_offset.yml",
    "line": 133,
    "summary": "The expected error message changed to 'must be an integer >= -1', but the test is still gated only on cluster_features gte_v7.12.0, with no new feature or capability for the new message.",
    "failure_scenario": "BWC and mixed-cluster YAML runs against older nodes return '[max_analyzed_offset] must be a positive integer', so the match assertion fails. There is also no YAML test for the new -1 behaviour behind a capability."
  },
  {
    "file": "server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/search/fetch/subphase/highlight/AbstractHighlighterBuilder.java",
    "line": 568,
    "summary": "Changing the check from `<= 0` to `< -1` makes max_analyzed_offset=0 valid, a behaviour change that looks unintended (the feature only needs -1).",
    "failure_scenario": "A request with max_analyzed_offset=0, rejected with 400 before, is now accepted. LimitTokenOffsetAnalyzer(0) then keeps only tokens starting at offset 0, and the index-limit check (0 > max is false) is skipped, so users silently get truncated or empty highlights instead of an error."
  },
  {
    "file": "server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/search/fetch/subphase/highlight/AbstractHighlighterBuilder.java",
    "line": 564,
    "summary": "The Javadoc says any 'negative int' defaults to the index offset, but validation rejects everything below -1; QueryMaxAnalyzedOffset.create also maps any `< 0` to the index value, so the three places disagree.",
    "failure_scenario": "Callers relying on the Javadoc pass -5 and get IllegalArgumentException. The mismatch also hides that 0 is now accepted as 'non-negative'."
  },
  {
    "file": "server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/lucene/search/uhighlight/QueryMaxAnalyzedOffset.java",
    "line": 16,
    "summary": "The constructor comment says 'If we have a negative value, grab value ... from the index', but the constructor only assigns the field; the substitution happens in create().",
    "failure_scenario": "A maintainer reads the comment, assumes the constructor normalises negatives, and adds another factory path that passes -1 straight through. LimitTokenOffsetAnalyzer(-1) would then drop every token."
  },
  {
    "file": "server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/lucene/search/uhighlight/CustomUnifiedHighlighter.java",
    "line": 145,
    "summary": "The error text (here and in PlainHighlighter) still tells users only to set max_analyzed_offset to a value below the index setting, and doesn't mention the new -1 option that is the simplest fix.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user hits the 'exceeds the [index.highlight.max_analyzed_offset] limit' error and has to guess a number, even though -1 would automatically truncate at the index limit."
  },
  {
    "file": "docs/reference/search/search-your-data/highlighting.asciidoc",
    "line": 1,
    "summary": "The public API contract changes (-1 means 'use the index setting', 0 is now accepted), but neither the highlighting docs nor a docs/changelog entry are updated.",
    "failure_scenario": "The documentation still says max_analyzed_offset must be a positive integer, so users never find the -1 option and the release notes leave out a user-visible API change."
  }
]
```
